---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { PLUMBING } from '../config';
import { graph, businessSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const jsonLd = graph(
  businessSchema(),
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'About', path: '/about/' },
  ]),
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About | English Handyman Berlin"
  description="British, 25 years in Berlin, qualified electrician with 15+ years in shopfitting and renovation. Who you're dealing with, what I cover, and where I bring someone else in."
  canonical="/about/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'About', path: '/about/' }]} />

  <article class="container-page pt-8">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-extrabold leading-tight text-ink-950 sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">
        Building your dreams and fixing your nightmares
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
        British, twenty-five years in Berlin, and a qualified electrician with fifteen years of
        shopfitting and renovation behind me.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-trade mt-12 max-w-3xl">
      {/* TODO: add the owner's name, and a photograph of him on site, once supplied. */}
      <h2>Who you're actually dealing with</h2>
      <p>
        Most people ring me after a bad experience. A quote that doubled. Someone who stopped
        answering the phone in week three. A kitchen fitted so badly the doors never lined up.
        It's rarely that the bloke couldn't do the work. It's that nobody was holding the job
        together, and nobody would give a straight answer in a language they spoke.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm British, I've been in Berlin twenty-five years, and I'm a qualified electrician with
        fifteen years of shopfitting and renovation behind me. You get the job explained in plain
        English, and dealt with in German where it has to be — the Hausverwaltung, the neighbours,
        the supplier who's lost your order. Shopfitting is where I learned to hit a fixed date and
        clean up behind myself, because a shop has to open on the day it says it will.
      </p>

      <h2>One person answering for it</h2>
      <p>
        I do the survey, the ordering, the sequencing and the work itself. On a bathroom that means
        the tiling doesn't start until the substrate is right, and the electrics go in before anyone
        closes a wall up. Obvious, but it's the thing that goes wrong most.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tiling I hand to a mate who does nothing else. There's a real difference between someone
        who tiles now and then and someone who tiles every day, and on big porcelain in a room where
        nothing is square you can see it from the doorway for the next twenty years.
      </p>

      <h2>Where I stop</h2>
      <p>
        On plumbing I do half of it. I'll fit a shower, a toilet, a basin or new taps onto pipework
        and drainage that's already where it needs to be — that's a normal part of a bathroom for
        me. What I don't do is {PLUMBING.notDoing}. Once the soil pipe or the supply has to move,
        that's a plumber, and I'll bring one in and work around them.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don't do architecture or structural engineering at all. Separate disciplines, separate
        liability, and anyone who tells you different is cutting a corner. Same approach: I get them
        in at the right point and keep everything running to one schedule, so you're not the one
        chasing three trades over WhatsApp.
      </p>
      <p>
        Knowing where the line is isn't a limitation. It's why the rest of it lands on time.
      </p>

      <h2>Berlin flats are their own problem</h2>
      <p>
        Twenty-five years here means very little surprises me. Walls out by centimetres rather than
        millimetres. Floors with sand and rubble fill between the joists. Lime plaster that won't
        hold a modern fixing. Circuits with no earth. Three-and-a-half metre ceilings with Stuck
        somebody has already half wrecked. None of it is a disaster, but it changes the price and
        the timeline, and you're better off hearing it before we start than in week two.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Tell me what you're dealing with"
    body="Send a photo and a sentence. I'll tell you whether it's a job for me, and what it involves."
  />
</BaseLayout>
